msgid "``2fa`` covers Weblate’s own second factor (TOTP, WebAuthn security keys, recovery codes through ``django_otp``), which the pinned image enables unconditionally and offers no setting for. While ``2fa`` is off, the ``javascript`` service of `meta/services.yml <./meta/services.yml>`__ is on and injects `templates/javascript.js.j2 <./templates/javascript.js.j2>`__, which hides the enrollment entries under ``/accounts/auth/tokens/`` in the account settings. Accounts that already enrolled keep their second factor at sign-in; nothing is enforced. ``login.method.2fa.enforced`` defaults to ``false`` and has no effect in this role, ``true`` included, because the role renders no enforcement: Weblate has no site-wide switch, only ``enforced_2fa`` per team or project, and a team with it drops its permissions for every member without a second factor on every request, API token requests included, while the role converges no team."
msgstr ""

msgid "``2fa`` is the TOTP second factor accounts enrol in the WebUI Account Manager. Stalwart offers it only under the Internal directory and has no server switch for it, so ``tasks/00_core.yml`` refuses a value that differs from “SSO off”. Stalwart cannot require the second factor either, so ``login.method.2fa.enforced`` only offers it here."
msgstr ""

msgid "``2fa``: GitLab CE offers its own second factor to every account and has no switch to withdraw it; while ``2fa`` is off, ``omniauth.allow_bypass_two_factor`` lets the Keycloak sign-in skip it, and the deploy refuses ``2fa: false`` without ``sso``. While ``2fa`` is on, ``login.method.2fa.enforced`` (default ``true``) sets the application setting ``require_two_factor_authentication`` with a ``two_factor_grace_period`` of ``0`` through `files/ruby/apply_login_methods.rb <./files/ruby/apply_login_methods.rb>`__; while ``2fa`` is off the deploy leaves both settings as an admin set them. With enforcement on, GitLab refuses the account password for Git over HTTPS and for the container registry, so every client needs a personal access token instead; personal access tokens keep working on the API."
msgstr ""

msgid "``2fa``: Odoo’s TOTP (``auth_totp``) has no switch that withdraws the offer, so the script hides the “Enable two-factor authentication” button of the user preferences while the method is off. While it is on, ``login.method.2fa.enforced`` (default ``true``) sets the system parameter ``auth_totp.policy`` to ``all_required``, which ``auth_totp_mail`` (installed with ``auth_totp`` and ``mail``) reads: an account without an authenticator app gets a code by mail at each sign-in, and password XML-RPC gives way to API keys. ``files/python/apply/two_factor_policy.py`` writes it on every deploy through ``odoo shell`` with ``set_param`` and signals the cache change to the running workers. It switches the enforcement on only while ``web-app-email`` is deployed and ``ir.mail_server`` opens an SMTP session at that deploy, and otherwise holds it and prints why; a policy it already switched on survives a failed SMTP probe. Switching it on stores the ownership marker ``infinito.auth_totp_policy_by_deploy`` with the policy it replaced. With ``enforced: false``, while ``2fa`` is off, or while the enforcement is held, it restores that replaced policy only while the marker is set and the policy still holds the deploy’s ``all_required``, so a policy an admin chose stays. The role’s own automation runs through ``odoo shell`` and SQL only. Break-glass for a locked-out administrator: ``odoo shell -d <database>``, then ``env['ir.config_parameter'].set_param('auth_totp.policy', False); env.cr.commit()``."
msgstr ""

msgid "``2fa``: ``[admin] USER_DISABLED_FEATURES=manage_mfa`` while off, so no account can enroll TOTP or security keys. While on, ``login.method.2fa.enforced: true`` sets ``[security] TWO_FACTOR_AUTH=enforced``, so an account without a second factor enrolls one before it gets in. The role’s default is ``false``, so Gitea only offers the second factor: enforcement takes repository access from every individual account without one, token requests included, and the MCP account the deploy provisions has none. An inventory that enforces it loses the MCP server’s repository tools. While ``2fa`` and ``passkey`` are both off, ``manage_mfa`` is disabled and the environment sets ``TWO_FACTOR_AUTH`` empty, because any enforcement would then lock out every account that cannot enroll a second factor. While ``2fa`` is off and ``passkey`` is on, the environment carries no ``TWO_FACTOR_AUTH``, so the deploy leaves that setting as an admin set it."
msgstr ""

